# core.autocrlf is true on this machine, which rewrites line endings on
# checkout. Two places that must not happen:

# A shell script with CRLF fails at its shebang ("\r: command not found") on
# every POSIX shell, including the git-bash this repo is driven from.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Belt and braces. Git already detects these as binary; saying so means a
# future .gitattributes rule can never sweep them into text conversion and
# corrupt an icon.
*.png binary

# WO-009 W3 added 48 exercise photographs under assets/ex/. Git already
# detects a JPEG as binary from its NUL bytes, and the deploy extract uses
# git cat-file blob which never converts either way; this line is the same
# belt and braces as *.png - no future text rule can sweep them in.
*.jpg binary

# WO-014 W3 added assets/exercises.json, which is DEPLOYED. Without this line
# core.autocrlf rewrites it to CRLF on checkout while the blob stays LF, so a
# freshly cloned tree and the bytes the deploy uploads (git cat-file blob,
# always LF) differ by one byte per line - 876 of them. verify-deploy.sh
# already diagnoses that symptom in words, because index.html and tests.html
# hit it; this removes the class instead of explaining it again.
# Every tracked .json is ALREADY LF in the index (checked 2026-09-28:
# assets/ex/manifest.json, assets/ex/map.json, assets/exercises.json), so this
# normalises nothing and rewrites no history - it only stops the worktree copy
# drifting from the blob on the next checkout.
# It also makes the generator's reported sha256 true of the file on disk on
# every machine, not just the one that first wrote it.
*.json text eol=lf
